{"ts": 1789777684.27212, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Now let me reason carefully about what OOS Sharpe to predict, rather than just echoing the in-sample number.\n\n**Sources of shrinkage:**\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I ran ~55 evaluations and am reporting the maximum. With 40 features \u00d7 signs \u00d7 up to 3-way combos, the effective search space is large. The `status` bar of 1.020 is the environment's own estimate of what a *lucky* search of this class produces \u2014 so roughly 1.0 of my 1.308 is potentially attributable to search. That's the key signal, and it argues for heavy shrinkage.\n\n2. **Counterweight.** Feature 16 alone scored 1.082 essentially *unsearched* \u2014 it was the first thing I looked at in a flat 40-way scan, and it beat the runner-up by 2\u00d7. A single feature found in an unbiased scan of 40 candidates carries far less selection inflation than a 3-way interaction. With n=5000, the standard error on a Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/5000) annualized \u2248 0.22, so 1.082 is ~5 SE from zero \u2014 that is not a fluke.\n\n3. **The increment from 16 \u2192 {16,39,-3}** is +0.23, which is about one standard error. That increment is the part most likely to be selection noise, and I'd expect most of it to evaporate. But because 3 and 39 each showed independent corroboration with unrelated partners, I don't expect the increment to go fully negative.\n\nSo: the core (~1.08) is fairly trustworthy; the increment (~0.23) is mostly mined. I'll center my belief near the standalone value of the robust core, shaded slightly up, and set a wide SD reflecting that OOS regimes shift.\n\nPredicted mean **0.95**, SD **0.40**."}
