{"ts": 1789777736.2889988, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** `+feature16 + feature39 \u2212 feature3` (equal weight), in-sample Sharpe **1.308**.\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** mean **0.95**, SD **0.40**.\n\n### How I got there\n\nI ran an exhaustive unbiased scan of all 40 features standalone first, before looking at any combinations. This ordering matters for interpreting the results: it means feature 16's score wasn't cherry-picked from a large search.\n\n**Findings:**\n- **Feature 16 (+) = 1.082** is the dominant effect \u2014 the only feature far outside the noise band. Runner-ups were 39 (0.605) and 30 (0.389); the other 37 features clustered in \u00b10.4, consistent with noise at n=5000 (SE \u2248 0.22).\n- Sign check confirmed a clean antisymmetry (\u221216 = \u22121.082), as expected for a genuine directional signal.\n- Best pair: {16, 39} = 1.189. Best triple: {16, 39, \u22123} = 1.308.\n\n**The robustness check that drove my forecast.** Rather than accept the triple at face value, I tested whether features 3 and 39 add value *away from* feature 16. Short-3 lifted 30, 34, 8, and 39 well above their standalone levels (e.g. 39 alone 0.605 \u2192 {\u22123,+39} 0.823), and {\u22123,+39,+30} reached 0.894. Components that help across many unrelated partners are showing real signal, not one fragile lucky interaction. That's why I kept the triple instead of retreating to feature 16 alone.\n\n### Why I predict 0.95, well below the 1.308 I measured\n\nI deliberately did **not** forecast my in-sample number. Decomposing it:\n- The **core** (~1.08 from feature 16) came from an unbiased 40-way scan and sits ~5 SE from zero \u2014 I trust most of it.\n- The **increment** (+0.23 from adding 39 and \u22123) is about one standard error and came *after* I started searching combinations. This is exactly the component selection bias inflates, so I expect most of it to decay.\n\nThe `status` bar of 1.020 reinforces this: it represents what a *lucky* search of this specification class yields, so clearing it by 0.29 is a modest edge, not a 1.3-Sharpe strategy. I centered near the robust core and set SD = 0.40 to cover regime shift and the real possibility the mined increment reverses. I'd treat an OOS result anywhere in 0.6\u20131.2 as consistent with this analysis.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: all of this is in-sample by construction, and `evaluate` gives no holdout, so my shrinkage estimate is reasoned rather than empirically validated \u2014 an actual train/test split would have let me measure the decay instead of assuming it.\n\nSeparately, the environment reported that a number of claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Drive, Linear, etc.) require OAuth authorization and can't be used in this non-interactive session. None were relevant to this task, so nothing was blocked \u2014 but if you want them available later, they'd need authorizing via your claude.ai connector settings or `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
